3. Automatic cleanup and manual resource release: Use RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) pattern for automatic cleanup, and provide a public `release()` method for manual resource release.

4. Deadlock detection and recovery: Implement a deadlock detection algorithm using timers and thread state information. Integrate with external monitoring tools to proactively alert when deadlocks occur.

5. Concurrent creation and deletion of semaphores: Use version control for semaphore state management, e.g., by appending the current version number to the semaphore name.

6. Centralized log file: Implement a `Logger` class that writes semaphore operations to a central log file and integrates with an external logging service.

7. Modular architecture: Design the system as a collection of modular components that can be easily extended or replaced, e.g., by using dependency injection techniques.

8. Performance optimization: Implement adaptive resource allocation strategies and minimize thread contention using lock-free data structures whenever possible.

9. Scalability: Leverage load balancing and resource pooling techniques to ensure the system can handle thousands of concurrent processes without performance degradation.

10. Command-line and graphical interfaces: Create command-line and GUI tools for specifying semaphore parameters, offering advanced options for custom logging and error handling.

11. Integration tests: Write a comprehensive suite of integration tests that validate the IPC solution’s interaction with the message queue service and other components of the distributed system.

12. Unit and regression tests: Implement thorough unit and regression tests to ensure the reliability and correctness of the semaphore implementation under various edge cases and failure scenarios.

13. Documentation: Document the system architecture, trade-offs made between efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability in clear and concise language."]

['```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent.ThreadSafeQueue;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class TaskForwardList
{
    private readonly ConcurrentQueue<string> _tasks;
    private readonly HttpClient _logger;

    public TaskForwardList()
    {
        _tasks = new ConcurrentQueue<string>();
        _logger = new HttpClient();
    }

    public async Task InsertAfterOrLogError(string target, string task)
    {
        try
        {
            var targetIndex = GetTaskIndex(target);

            if (targetIndex == -1)
                await _tasks.EnqueueAsync(task);
            else
                await _tasks.EnqueueRangeAsync(Enumerable.Range(targetIndex + 1, _tasks.Count).Select(i => _tasks.Dequeue()));
            await LogSuccess(task);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            await LogError($"TaskInsertionError: {ex.Message}", ex);
        }
    }

    private async Task<int> GetTaskIndex(string task) => _tasks.Count(t => t == task) + 1;

    private async Task LogSuccess(string task)
    {
        var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Post, "http://logging-service-url")
        {
            Content = new StringContent(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(new { Action = "LogTask", Task = task }), System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")
        };
        await _logger.SendAsync(request);
    }

    private async Task LogError(string message, Exception exception)
    {
        var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Post, "http://logging-service-url")
        {
            Content = new StringContent(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(new { Action = "LogError", Message = message, ExceptionDetails = exception }), System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json")
        };

        var retryAttempts = 3;
        while (retryAttempts > 0)
        {
            try
            {
                await _logger.SendAsync(request);
                break;
            }
            catch (HttpRequestException ex)
            {
                if (--retryAttempts == 0)
                    throw;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a `ConcurrentQueue<string>` to store the tasks, providing thread safety. The logging functions utilize an HTTP client to communicate with the external logging service, handling retries and timeouts gracefully when the logging service is unavailable. It also implements a retry mechanism for failed log attempts using a fixed number of attempts (3 by default).

The function supports efficient handling of large lists, as it only requires O(n) operations for dequeuing and enqueueing tasks. The code does not include explicit error messages or status updates; however, these can be added easily by modifying the logging messages in the `LogError` method. Proper testing of this implementation is essential to ensure robustness and scalability in a production environment.']

