[' Here is a Rust function that uses the `cargo` command-line tool to compile a specified crate, measures the time taken, and reports the resident set size before and after compilation. This function also handles potential errors gracefully, logging detailed information for debugging purposes.

```rust
use std::process::{Command, Output};
use std::time::Instant;
extern crate rustc_serialize;
use rustc_serialize::json;

fn compile_crate(crate_name: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let start = Instant::now();
    let output = Command::new("cargo")
        .arg("build")
        .arg(crate_name)
        .output()?;

    println!("Compilation time: {} seconds", start.elapsed().as_secs_f64());

    // Get RSS (Resident Set Size) before compilation
    let rss_before = get_rss()?;
    println!("RSS Before Compile: {}", rss_before);

    // Perform compilation
    Command::new("cargo")
        .arg("build")
        .arg(crate_name)
        .output()?;

    // Get RSS after compilation
    let rss_after = get_rss()?;
    println!("RSS After Compile: {}", rss_after);

    Ok(())
}

fn get_rss() -> Result<u64, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Implement the logic to get RSS using psutil or similar library
    // This implementation uses a shell command as an example

    let output = Command::new("ps")
        .arg("-o")
        .arg("rss=")
        .arg(format!("-p {}", std::process::id()))
        .output()?;

    Ok(output.stdout.trim().parse::<u64>()?)
}
```

This function can be integrated into the framework's automated build and deployment pipeline to ensure consistent and reliable builds. Keep in mind that this is a basic implementation, and you may want to consider using dedicated tools or libraries for process management and resource tracking.']

