[' I'm afraid as a text-based AI model, I can't directly write or execute code, but I can certainly help you design the structure and guide you on how to implement it in Rust. Here is an outline of the `compile_with_custom_hooks` function and its dependencies within the `BuildKit` library:

```rust
pub struct BuildKit {
    // Handles different build configurations and dependencies
    builder: Box<dyn BuildSystem>,

    // Logging configuration and hooks
    log: Logger,

    // Metrics tracking for CPU time, memory consumption, etc.
    metrics: Metrics,
}

impl BuildKit {
    pub fn new(builder: Box<dyn BuildSystem>, logger: Logger) -> Self {
        Self { builder, log: logger, metrics: Metrics::new() }
    }

    pub fn compile_with_custom_hooks(&mut self, project_dir: &str) -> Result<CompilationReport, CompilationError> {
        // Ensure a clean build environment and set up logging hooks

        self.log.info("Starting compilation for {}", project_dir);

        // Start a new scope for metrics collection
        let timer = Instant::now();
        let mem_before = MemStats::new().unwrap();

        match self.builder.build(project_dir) {
            Ok(_) => {
                self.log.info("Compilation succeeded.");
                // Collect metrics and create the report
                let elapsed = timer.elapsed().as_secs_f64();
                let mem_delta = MemStats::new().unwrap() - mem_before;
                CompilationReport {
                    success: true,
                    cpu_time: elapsed,
                    memory_consumption: mem_delta,
                    errors: vec![],
                }
            },
            Err(e) => {
                self.log.error(&format!("Compilation failed: {}", e));
                // Collect metrics and create the report with error information
                let elapsed = timer.elapsed().as_secs_f64();
                let mem_delta = MemStats::new().unwrap() - mem_before;
                CompilationReport {
                    success: false,
                    cpu_time: elapsed,
                    memory_consumption: mem_delta,
                    errors: vec![e],
                }
            },
        }
    }
}
```

This is a high-level design of the function. You would need to implement the `BuildSystem`, `Logger`, `Metrics`, `CompilationReport`, and `CompilationError` types as well as their respective functionalities.

For managing memory usage in large projects, consider using `VecDeque` for buffering log entries instead of appending them directly to a string, and use lazy_static or once_cell for global variables.

Regarding log verbosity vs performance, you could implement different log levels (info, debug, warn, error) and provide an option to filter the logs based on the desired level. This would allow users to adjust the logging level according to their needs without impacting the overall performance of the library.

To ensure compatibility across different operating systems and versions of build tools, you could utilize cross-platform crates such as `tempfile` for creating temporary files and directories, and use features flags or conditional compilation to support multiple versions of the build tools.

Lastly, consider using a crate like `lazy_static` or `once_cell` for global variables to ensure thread-safety when dealing with concurrent builds.']

