- Choose Keycloak for OAuth 2.0 integration due to its resilience, affordability, and support for asynchronous operations. Implement retry logic using a library like `reqwest_retry` with exponential backoff. In case of service disruptions, implement a fallback strategy using an in-memory cache for temporarily storing tokens.

```rust
use reqwest::Client;
use reqwest_retry::RetryPolicy;
use reqwest_retry::{self as retry, Error};

let client = Client::new();
let retry_policy = RetryPolicy::standard_with_config(Default::default(), |error| {
    if matches!(error.kind(), Error::Timeout) || error.is_server_error() {
        true
    } else {
        false
    }
});
let token_url = "https://your-keycloak-instance/auth/realms/your-realm/protocol/openid-connect/token";
async fn get_token(client: &Client, refresh_token: &str) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {
    retry::retry_with(retry_policy.into(), |attempt| {
        async move {
            client.post(token_url).form(&[("grant_type", "refresh_token"), ("refresh_token", refresh_token)])
                .send()
                .await?
                .text()
                .await
        }
    })
    .await
}
```

2. Distributed Locking and Contention:

- Use Redis for distributed locking, implementing a read-write lock strategy to handle contention during peak loads.

```rust
use redis::Client;
use redis::commands::set::SetArgs;
use redis::AsyncCommands;

async fn acquire_lock(client: &Client, key: &str) -> Result<(), redis::RedisError> {
    let mut lock = client.get(key).expect("Failed to create lock");
    if lock.exists()? {
        return Err(redis::RedisError::DataError);
    } else {
        lock.set(0, Some(SetArgs::new().nx()))?;
        Ok(())
    }
}
async fn release_lock(client: &Client, key: &str) -> Result<(), redis::RedisError> {
    client.del(key)?;
    Ok(())
}
```

3. Token Revocation:

- Implement a background job queue using RabbitMQ for asynchronous token revocation.

```rust
use amqp::{Channel, Connection};
use rabbitmq_stream::consumer::{Consumer, DeliverOnce};

async fn send_revoke_message(queue: &str, token: &str) {
    let (mut conn, channel) = connect("amqp://your-rabbitmq-instance").await.unwrap();
    channel.basic_publish("", queue, "", false, vec![token.as_bytes()]).unwrap();
}
async fn revoke_tokens(consumer: &Consumer<DeliverOnce>) {
    loop {
        if let Ok((delivery, _) ) = consumer.next().await {
            let token = String::from_utf8(delivery.body()).unwrap();
            // Revoke the token and cleanup
        }
    }
}
```

4. Data Storage and Caching:

- Store tokens in a distributed key-value store like Amazon DynamoDB with read replicas, cache frequently accessed tokens using Redis for improved response times.

```rust
use aws_sdk_dynamodb::{AttributeValue, Client as DynamoClient};
use redis::Client;

async fn get_token_from_db(tenant_id: &str) -> Option<String> {
    let client = DynamoClient::new();
    let response = client.get_item()
        .table_name("Tokens")
        .key("tenantId").equal_to(tenant_id)
        .expression_attribute_names(Some(serde_json::json! { {"token": "token"}}))
        .expression_attribute_values(Some(serde_json::json! { {{"token": AttributeValue::S("") } }}))
        .projection_expression("#token")
        .execute().await?;
    response.item().and_then(|item| item.get("token").map(|v| v.to_string()))
}
async fn store_token(tenant_id: &str, token: &str) {
    let client = DynamoClient::new();
    let item = serde_json::json! {{ "tenantId": tenant_id, "token": token }};
    client.put_item()
        .table_name("Tokens")
        .item(item)
        .execute().await?;
}
```

