[" Below is a high-level design and an example implementation outline for the `DynamicEventProcessor` service in C#. Please note that this example does not include specific external dependencies or implementations; it serves as a starting point to build upon.

```csharp
namespace DynamicEventProcessor
{
    public interface IExternalSource
    {
        Task<IEnumerable<Event>> GetEventsAsync();
    }

    public abstract class ExternalDataSource : IExternalSource
    {
        // Base class for handling connection pooling and retries.
    }

    public interface IEventProcessor
    {
        Task ProcessAsync(Event e);
    }

    public abstract class EventProcessor : IEventProcessor
    {
        protected virtual Task ProcessInternalAsync(Event e) => throw new NotImplementedException();

        public async Task ProcessAsync(Event e)
        {
            await ProcessInternalAsync(e).ConfigureAwait(false);

            // Implement caching strategies for frequently accessed data here.
        }
    }

    public class DynamicEventProcessorService
    {
        private readonly IEnumerable<IExternalSource> _dataSources;
        private readonly IEnumerable<IEventProcessor> _eventProcessors;
        private readonly IRuleEngine _ruleEngine;

        public DynamicEventProcessorService(IEnumerable<IExternalSource> dataSources,
                                             IEnumerable<IEventProcessor> eventProcessors,
                                             IRuleEngine ruleEngine)
        {
            _dataSources = dataSources;
            _eventProcessors = eventProcessors;
            _ruleEngine = ruleEngine;
        }

        public async Task RunAsync()
        {
            var events = await FetchAndAggregateEventsAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);

            foreach (var eventProcessor in _eventProcessors)
            {
                // Apply processing policies using the rule engine.
                await ProcessEventsWithPoliciesAsync(events, eventProcessor).ConfigureAwait(false);
            }
        }

        private async Task<IEnumerable<Event>> FetchAndAggregateEventsAsync()
        {
            var tasks = _dataSources.Select(ds => ds.GetEventsAsync());
            var events = await Task.WhenAll(tasks).ConfigureAwait(false);

            // Implement statistical anomaly detection here.
            return AggregateEvents(events);
        }

        private async Task ProcessEventsWithPoliciesAsync(IEnumerable<Event> events, IEventProcessor processor)
        {
            var eventGroups = _ruleEngine.GroupEvents(events);
            foreach (var group in eventGroups)
            {
                await ProcessGroupAsync(group, processor).ConfigureAwait(false);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This outline provides a starting point for implementing the described requirements. The `DynamicEventProcessorService` is designed to be modular and scalable, with the ability to integrate with various external data sources (`IExternalSource`) and processors (`IEventProcessor`). It also includes a rule engine for configuring processing policies dynamically.

The design allows for asynchronous event processing, error handling, and caching strategies while maintaining low latency and resource efficiency. To achieve this, you'll need to further implement the necessary details for each interface, such as connection pooling, retries, fallback mechanisms, and statistical anomaly detection.

As part of the development process, consider implementing monitoring, telemetry, and logging solutions to optimize performance and manage resource consumption effectively. Additionally, leverage Kubernetes and cloud-based auto-scaling policies for horizontal scaling based on metrics like event ingestion rate, queue depth, and processing time."]

