[' Here's a high-level structure for the `DynamicFileDataProcessor` library in C#. This is not complete or production-ready code, but it should give you a good starting point to build upon.

```csharp
namespace DynamicFileDataProcessor
{
    public interface IDataFormatter<T>
    {
        Task<List<T>> DeserializeAsync(Stream stream);
        Stream SerializeAsync(IEnumerable<T> data);
    }

    public abstract class FileDataGenerator<T>
    {
        protected readonly IDataFormatter<T> DataFormatter;
        protected readonly ICacheManager CacheManager;
        protected readonly string FileFormat;
        protected readonly object LockObject = new object();

        protected FileDataGenerator(IDataFormatter<T> dataFormatter, ICacheManager cacheManager, string fileFormat)
        {
            DataFormatter = dataFormatter;
            CacheManager = cacheManager;
            FileFormat = fileFormat;
        }

        public virtual async Task<List<T>> ReadAsync(string fileName)
        {
            // Implement caching here
            return await CacheManager.GetOrAddAsync(fileName, async () =>
            {
                using var stream = new FileStream(fileName, FileMode.Open);
                return await DataFormatter.DeserializeAsync(stream);
            });
        }

        public virtual List<T> ReadSync(string fileName)
        {
            // Implement caching here
            var cachedData = CacheManager.Get(fileName);
            if (cachedData != null)
                return (List<T>)cachedData;

            using var stream = new FileStream(fileName, FileMode.Open);
            return DataFormatter.Deserialize(stream).ToList();
        }

        public virtual async Task GenerateAsync(string fileName, Func<Task<List<T>>> dataGenerator)
        {
            await CacheManager.RemoveAsync(fileName);
            using var stream = new FileStream(fileName, FileMode.CreateNew);
            await DataFormatter.SerializeAsync(await dataGenerator());
        }

        public virtual void GenerateSync(string fileName, Func<List<T>> dataGenerator)
        {
            CacheManager.Remove(fileName);
            using var stream = new FileStream(fileName, FileMode.CreateNew);
            DataFormatter.Serialize(dataGenerator()).WriteTo(stream);
        }
    }

    public class CsvFileDataGenerator : FileDataGenerator<List<dynamic>>
    {
        public CsvFileDataGenerator(IDataFormatter<List<dynamic>> csvDataFormatter, ICacheManager cacheManager)
            : base(csvDataFormatter, cacheManager, "CSV") { }
    }

    public class JsonFileDataGenerator : FileDataGenerator<List<T>>
    {
        public JsonFileDataGenerator(IDataFormatter<List<T>> jsonDataFormatter, ICacheManager cacheManager)
            : base(jsonDataFormatter, cacheManager, "JSON") { }
    }
}
```

This code defines an abstract class `FileDataGenerator` with read and generate methods for both synchronous and asynchronous operations. It also implements a caching mechanism using an `ICacheManager`. You'll need to implement concrete classes like `CsvFileDataGenerator` and `JsonFileDataGenerator` that inherit from the base class and handle specific file formats.

You may want to consider using a library like `System.IO.Pipelines` for efficient stream processing, and a caching library such as `Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory` for handling caching. You can also use a logging framework like Serilog or NLog to implement detailed logging.

To ensure testability, consider creating unit tests for each class method, covering various edge cases and error scenarios. To handle external data sources effectively, you might want to consider implementing additional data generators that can read from databases, APIs, etc., and provide a unified interface with the `FileDataGenerator` base class.']

[" Due to the complexity of the task, I'll provide a high-level overview of the structure for `upgradeDatabaseSchema` function in Node.js:

```javascript
const { SchemaVersionAPI, MessageBroker } = require('third-party-libraries');
const cache = require('in-memory-data-structure');
const analyticsPipeline = require('./analyticsPipeline');
const lockManager = require('./lockManager');

async function upgradeDatabaseSchema() {
  try {
    // Fetch current schema version and tenant metadata from API
    const { currentVersion, tenantMetadata } = await SchemaVersionAPI.getCurrentVersion();

    // Check if an upgrade is necessary
    if (currentVersion === TARGET_SCHEMA_VERSION) return;

    // Start a transaction to ensure data integrity during the upgrade
    await database.startTransaction();

    // Define the migration steps as functions
    const migrations = [
      migrateStep1,
      migrateStep2,
      // Add more migration steps as needed
    ];

    // Execute each migration step concurrently while managing lock contention and retries
    await Promise.all(migrations.map(async (migration) => {
      try {
        // Lock the tenant during the migration to prevent concurrent writes
        await lockManager.acquireLock(tenantMetadata);

        // Execute the migration step
        await migration();

        // Release the lock after the migration is complete
        await lockManager.releaseLock(tenantMetadata);
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Error during migration ${migration.name}:`, error);
        if (!lockManager.isLocked(tenantMetadata)) {
          // Retry the migration step if not already locked by another process
          await Promise.delay(RETRY_DELAY);
          return upgradeDatabaseSchema();
        }
      }
    }));

    // Commit the transaction after all migrations are complete
    await database.commit();

    // Notify affected tenants and start asynchronous data migrations
    await MessageBroker.sendNotification('data_migration_started', tenantMetadata);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error during upgrade:`, error);

    // Rollback the transaction in case of a failure
    if (database.isInTransaction) await database.rollback();

    // Retry the upgrade after a delay if necessary, handling partial upgrades and rollbacks
    await Promise.delay(RETRY_DELAY);
    return upgradeDatabaseSchema();
  } finally {
    // Cleanup and release resources
    database.end();
  }
}
```

This code serves as a starting point for implementing the desired functionality. You will need to fill in the details, such as adding the migration steps (`migrateStep1`, `migrateStep2`, etc.), implementing the lock manager, and integrating with the analytics pipeline and message broker. Additionally, performance optimizations, security configurations, testing strategies, and monitoring should be implemented according to your project requirements."]

